WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:13.810
<v Nancy Updike>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Nancy Updike, and I'm filling in for Ira Glass, who's working on a great, action-heavy, live stage show. He will be back next week.
<v Nancy Updike>这里是芝加哥 WBEZ 电台的《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是南希·厄普代克，我正在为艾拉·格拉斯代班，他目前正在筹备一场精彩、充满动作元素的现场演出。他下周就会回来。

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:29.670
<v Nancy Updike>And in the meantime, I wanted to share this story. It's a little personal. I was at MAC-- the makeup store, not the computer store-- and I was buying foundation, which I almost never wear. That's the makeup you put all over your face to give yourself pretend perfect skin.
<v Nancy Updike>在此期间，我想分享这个故事。它有点私人。我当时在 MAC——是化妆品店，不是电脑店——我在买粉底，我几乎从不用它。那是你涂在整张脸上，让自己看起来皮肤完美无瑕的化妆品。

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:57.360
<v Nancy Updike>And I asked the salesman for help finding the right color. And he looked at me and said-- almost like he was thinking out loud-- he said, your neck, it's so much more yellow than your face. And then he turned away to start looking for the impossible color that would solve this problem of the yellow right next to the so much more yellow.
<v Nancy Updike>我请售货员帮忙找合适的颜色。他看着我说——就像在自言自语一样——他说，你的脖子比你的脸黄太多了。然后他转过身去，开始寻找那种不可能存在的颜色，来解决这个紧挨着明显更黄的肤色的黄度问题。

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:29.130
<v Nancy Updike>And if you're thinking, oh, this was just a sales technique to invent a problem and then offer to fix it with more products, I wish that that had been the case. But this was not an upsell. This was a cri de couer. The man really just seemed to be expressing his frustration at this stumper of my mismatched face and neck.
<v Nancy Updike>如果你认为，哦，这只是发明一个问题然后推销更多产品来解决它的销售技巧，我倒希望是这样。但这并不是为了促销，而是一种发自内心的感慨。那个人似乎真的在为我这不协调的脸和脖子带来的难题感到沮丧。

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:56.010
<v Nancy Updike>This sort of out-of-the-blue, perfectly sharpened comment stops you cold because it's not an insult. It's an observation that is true. You just hadn't thought of it before. It's shocking because you think, I know myself. I know what I've got, what I haven't got. No one's going to spot something about me that I haven't already seen. Not true. You can be among friends, doing something you love, feeling great--
<v Nancy Updike>这种突如其来的、精准犀利的评论会让你瞬间僵住，因为它不是侮辱，而是一种事实观察。你只是之前没想过而已。这很令人震惊，因为你会想，我了解我自己。我知道我有什么，没什么。没人能发现我还没注意到的关于我自己的东西。事实并非如此。你可以和朋友在一起，做着你喜欢的事，感觉棒极了——

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.640
<v Dee Watson>We were backstage, you know, getting ready to go on, preparing.
<v Dee Watson>我们当时在后台，你知道，准备登台，正在候场。

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.160
<v Nancy Updike>This is Dee Watson. She was in a play a few weeks ago.
<v Nancy Updike>这是迪·沃森。她几周前演了一部戏。

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:28.810
<v Dee Watson>And it was all black women. Most of us were a little bit bigger than the average, you know. And it was a supportive atmosphere. And the subject of big behinds came up. And me having one, I know all about it. And one of the younger cast members-- she was about 25-- said, my mother thought your butt was so big it had to be a prop.
<v Dee Watson>全都是黑人女性。我们大多数人都比一般人胖一点，你知道的。那是种很支持彼此的氛围。然后话题转到了大屁股上。作为一个有大屁股的人，我对此很了解。其中一个年轻的演员——她大约 25 岁——说，我妈妈以为你的屁股太大，一定是塞了道具。

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.910
<v Nancy Updike>Oh my god.
<v Nancy Updike>天哪。

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:50.370
<v Dee Watson>And at that moment, I didn't hear anything else anybody said. I just was-- that was echoing through my head, and I could hear everybody laughing. And oh my gosh, that's so funny. And I'm standing there. I wanted to cry. I really wanted to cry. And I really don't think this young girl really meant to hurt me.
<v Dee Watson>那一刻，我没听见别人说的任何话。我只是——那句话在我的脑海里回响，我能听到每个人都在笑。天哪，那太好笑了。我站在那里。我想哭。我真的想哭。而且我真的不认为这个年轻女孩是故意想伤害我。

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.460
<v Nancy Updike>Well, and maybe it's like, you know, we're all women, so it's safe here to say anything.
<v Nancy Updike>嗯，可能就像是，你知道，我们都是女人，所以在这里说什么都安全。

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:09.590
<v Dee Watson>Yeah. I think she thought I would think that was funny, and I did not.
<v Dee Watson>是啊。我觉得她以为我会觉得那很好笑，但我并没有。

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:26.660
<v Nancy Updike>These are not statements that a human being forgets. The moment you hear the observation, it becomes part of how you see yourself, seemingly forever. Even something tiny, if it hits you right, can turn into this chirpy little voicemail that your brain is never able to erase.
<v Nancy Updike>这些都是人类无法忘记的话。一旦你听到这种观察，它就成了你看待自己方式的一部分，似乎永远如此。即使是一些微小的事情，如果它触动了你，它就会变成那种你的大脑永远无法抹去的喋喋不休的语音留言。

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:42.790
<v Nancy Updike>And it doesn't have to be about looks. It can be a comment on how you run or laugh or drive, how much money you make, what books you've read or haven't read-- any outside assessment of you that you never saw coming and could not shake once it was uttered.
<v Nancy Updike>而且这不一定非得是关于外貌。它可以是对你跑步、大笑或开车方式的评价，你挣多少钱，你看过或没看过什么书——任何你意想不到且一旦说出口就无法摆脱的外部评价。

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:56.230
<v Nancy Updike>One of our former producers, Jane Feltes-- she's actually now Jane Marie-- years ago dated a man who she later decided was a jerk. He wanted to make her feel insecure. But one night they were watching TV, and her feet were sticking up out of the blanket.
<v Nancy Updike>我们的一位前制作人简·费尔特斯——她现在叫简·玛丽——多年前和一个后来被她认为是混蛋的男人约会。他想让她感到自卑。但有一天晚上，他们正在看电视，她的脚从毯子里伸了出来。

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:17.600
<v Jane Feltes>And he turned to me, and he said, oh, you have juice box toes. And I was like, what? And he said, yeah, like Fred Flintstone feet. He's a caveman-- literally a caveman. A cartoon of a caveman-- a chubby, squat cartoon of a caveman.
<v Jane Feltes>他转向我，说，哦，你有果汁盒脚趾。我说，什么？他说，是啊，就像弗莱德·弗林石的脚。他是穴居人——真的是个穴居人。一个穴居人的卡通画——一个肥胖、矮蹲的卡通穴居人。

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:33.720
<v Jane Feltes>And I know it sounds so stupid, because who cares what shape my toes are? But you do want the person that you're in love with to just think that every part of you is amazing and beautiful-- or shut up about it. Or shut up about it.
<v Jane Feltes>我知道这听起来很蠢，因为谁在乎脚趾是什么形状？但你确实希望你深爱的人认为你身上的每一部分都是迷人而美丽的——或者对此闭嘴。或者对此闭嘴。

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:47.160
<v Jane Feltes>Maybe they're ugly. I don't know. Now that I'm looking at them-- I don't know. My big toe is definitely square.
<v Jane Feltes>也许它们很难看。我不知道。现在我看着它们——我不知道。我的大脚趾确实是方的。

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.030
<v Matthew Dicks>When I get shy kids, one of the things I do with them is I try to give them permission to make fun of me.
<v Matthew Dicks>当我遇到害羞的孩子时，我做的其中一件事就是试着给他们开玩笑戏弄我的许可。

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:58.390
<v Nancy Updike>This is a fifth grade teacher named Matthew Dicks, who is either the bravest or the most foolhardy man in America.
<v Nancy Updike>这是一位名叫马修·迪克斯的五年级老师，他要么是美国最勇敢的人，要么是最鲁莽的人。

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:02.550
<v Matthew Dicks>And they often come out of  their shells by becoming the person who can taunt the teacher.
<v Matthew Dicks>他们往往通过成为那个可以嘲弄老师的人而走出自己的壳。

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.600
<v Nancy Updike>That sounds very dangerous.
<v Nancy Updike>听起来很危险。

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:10.660
<v Matthew Dicks>It is. I mean, you have to teach them where the line is. And sometimes I don't know where the line is either.
<v Matthew Dicks>确实。我是说，你必须教他们界限在哪里。有时连我自己都不知道界限在哪里。

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:18.490
<v Nancy Updike>A few years ago, there was a girl in his class, very shy. And she got the nod that it was OK for her to make fun of Matthew if she wanted.
<v Nancy Updike>几年前，他班上有个女孩，非常害羞。她得到了可以嘲弄马修的许可。

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:30.920
<v Matthew Dicks>So one day she came in, and she just walked in very casually. And she looked at me and she said, hi, Jerry. And I looked at her and I said, Jerry? And she said, never mind. And she just walked away.
<v Matthew Dicks>所以有一天她进来了，走得很随意。她看着我说，嗨，杰里。我看着她说，杰里？她说，没事。然后就走开了。

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:43.900
<v Matthew Dicks>And I knew she was setting me up for some joke. And it went on for days. Every time she'd walk by me, she'd say, hey Jerry, how's it going?
<v Matthew Dicks>我知道她是在给我下套。这持续了好几天。每次她从我身边走过，她都会说，嘿，杰里，怎么样？

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:57.030
<v Matthew Dicks>And so finally, after about a week, I couldn't take it anymore. And she came in one morning and she said, how you doing, Jerry? And I said, fine, who is Jerry? And she said, Jerry's your bald spot.
<v Matthew Dicks>所以最后，大约一周后，我实在受不了了。一天早上她进来，她说，你好吗，杰里？我说，很好，谁是杰里？她说，杰里是你的秃顶。

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.240
<v Nancy Updike>Yikes.
<v Nancy Updike>天哪。

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:20.100
<v Matthew Dicks>And I tried to play it off like, I don't have a bald spot. Go sit down. Give me a break. Ha ha. That was, like, the most ridiculous week-long joke I've ever heard in my life. But as soon as my kids left the room to go to gym, I ran to the bathroom, and I leaned over the sink. And at the very top of my head, I had a bald spot that I had no idea about.
<v Matthew Dicks>我试图掩饰说，我没有秃顶。去坐下。饶了我吧。哈哈。那是我这辈子听过的最荒谬的长达一周的笑话。但当我的学生们离开教室去上体育课时，我跑到洗手间，俯身在洗手池上。在我的头顶处，真的有一块我根本不知道的秃顶。

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:26.640
<v Nancy Updike>Can I ask a logistical question? How does a fifth grader spot the top of your head?
<v Nancy Updike>我可以问个逻辑问题吗？五年级的学生怎么会看到你的头顶？

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:40.700
<v Matthew Dicks>Oh. So in my classroom, I teach some Shakespeare to my students. And we have a stage in my classroom. I built a stage with curtains and lighting and everything. So if you're standing on my stage in my classroom, you can look down on me.
<v Matthew Dicks>哦。在我的教室里，我给学生们教一些莎士比亚的东西。我的教室里有一个舞台。我搭了一个带有幕布、灯光和一切设施的舞台。所以如果你站在我的舞台上，你就能俯视我。

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:56.850
<v Nancy Updike>Now, you can say that Matthew brought this on himself. He gives some students permission to make fun of him, and he built a stage where students can peer down at him in judgment. But even if he did none of that, he's still a sitting duck for this exact kind of critical gaze.
<v Nancy Updike>现在，你可以说这是马修自找的。他给了一些学生嘲弄他的许可，他还搭了一个舞台，让学生们可以审视地俯视他。但即使他没做那些事，他依然是这种审视目光的活靶子。

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.020
<v Nancy Updike>You have 20 people-- you have 20 students, would you say?
<v Nancy Updike>你有 20 个人——你说有 20 个学生？

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.890
<v Matthew Dicks>Yeah, between 20 and 30 every year.
<v Matthew Dicks>是啊，每年在 20 到 30 个人之间。

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:12.710
<v Nancy Updike>OK, so between 20 and 30 people looking at you all day, every work day, and just taking stock of you.
<v Nancy Updike>好吧，也就是说每天工作时间都有 20 到 30 个人在盯着你看，并对你进行审视。

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:27.210
<v Matthew Dicks>Yes, it's constant. I had pinkeye a couple of weeks ago. The kids knew I had pinkeye before I did, you know, because they just stare at you all day. And they see any sort of minute change in you. You know, you're really the only thing they look at for a great majority of the day.
<v Matthew Dicks>是的，一直都是这样。我几周前得了红眼病。孩子们比我先知道，你知道，因为他们整天盯着你看。他们能看出你任何细微的变化。你知道，在一天的大部分时间里，你确实是他们唯一看的东西。

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:41.520
<v Matthew Dicks>So they notice these little things. If I get a new shirt, they immediately notice. Everything that changes about me, they notice right away.
<v Matthew Dicks>所以他们注意到了这些小事。如果我穿了一件新衬衫，他们马上就会注意到。关于我的一切变化，他们都会立刻注意到。

00:07:41.520 --> 00:08:15.380
<v Nancy Updike>Today on the radio show, I, Nancy "Yellow Neck" Updike bring you stories about people facing the unexpected moment of realizing how other people see them, what that means, and what in God's name to do about it. Today's show "Is That What I Look Like?" Prepare to learn the truth. Plus, a special guest appearance that will surprise and delight you. Stay with us.
<v Nancy Updike>今天在电台节目中，我，南希·“黄脖子”·厄普代克，将为你带来一些故事，讲述人们在面对意想不到的时刻，意识到别人是如何看待他们，那意味着什么，以及到底该怎么办。今天的节目是“我看起来是那个样子吗？”准备好了解真相吧。另外，还有一个会让你感到惊喜和愉悦的特别嘉宾。请继续收听。

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:24.030
<v Nancy Updike>Act One, Blunt Force. Domingo Martinez has this story about a vision in Brownsville, Texas. Here's Domingo.
<v Nancy Updike>第一幕，直截了当的打击。多明戈·马丁内斯讲述了这个关于德克萨斯州布朗斯维尔的愿景的故事。请听多明戈的讲述。

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:40.970
<v Domingo Martinez>When I was 16, I realized as far as my family went, school was considered my time, which meant I couldn't be pressed into labor by my father or grandmother. They were farm workers, and they made no claim on my time when I was supposed to be in school. So I learned to take advantage of this.
<v Domingo Martinez>我 16 岁时意识到，就我们家而言，学校被认为是我的时间，这意味着父亲或祖母不能强迫我劳动。他们是农场工人，当我知道该在学校时，他们不会占用我的时间。所以我学会了利用这一点。

00:08:40.970 --> 00:09:03.780
<v Domingo Martinez>I'd make it to school before 7:30 AM, either by school bus or my mother's Taurus, and then wait out options for escape. By my sophomore year, this kid named Tony Garcia had become my primary friend. Tony was nearly 19 and only a junior, but he didn't seem all that bad because he had good parents and an even better little brother, who was about to lap him at graduation.
<v Domingo Martinez>我会在早上 7:30 之前通过校车或我母亲的金牛座汽车赶到学校，然后等待逃跑的机会。到了高二，这个叫托尼·加西亚的家伙成了我的主要朋友。托尼快 19 岁了才上高二，但他看起来并不坏，因为他有很好的父母，还有一个即将要在毕业时超过他的更棒的弟弟。

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:32.560
<v Domingo Martinez>Together, Tony and I would find ways to while away the hours by doing anything other than attending class before we had to report home again. We were big fans of Huckleberry Finn and Tom Sawyer, and we believed we were continuing a long celebrated American tradition by ditching class and getting stoned, a fantasy combination of Mark Twain and Hunter S. Thompson. But really, we were just lazy and looking for a good time.
<v Domingo Martinez>我和托尼会想方设法消磨时间，做除了上课之外的任何事，直到我们不得不报告回家。我们是《哈克贝利·费恩历险记》和《汤姆·索亚历险记》的忠实粉丝，我们相信通过逃课和吸食大麻，我们是在延续一种备受推崇的美国传统，这是一种马克·吐温和亨特·S·汤普森风格的幻想结合。但说实话，我们只是懒惰，想找点乐子。

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:53.310
<v Domingo Martinez>The skipping itself was not a problem. The problem was taking care of our grades and absences on our report cards before they reached our parents. And it was actually Tony who initiated me into the trade that I'd eventually pursue, graphic design. But in this early stage in high school, it was plain and simple forgery. There was no design in what we were doing.
<v Domingo Martinez>逃课本身不是问题。问题是在成绩单传到父母手中之前处理好我们的成绩和缺勤记录。实际上是托尼引导我进入了那个我最终从事的行业：平面设计。但在高中早期，那只是明目张胆的伪造。我们所做的一切毫无设计可言。

00:09:53.310 --> 00:10:21.810
<v Domingo Martinez>It was Tony who placed the first X-Acto knife I ever held in my hands, and immediately I felt an overwhelming sense of possibility holding that little penknife. We'd intercept the report cards when they came in the mail. And then Tony took careful pains to explain this whole process to me, his flunky-- a term that came uncomfortably close to becoming literal-- over the photocopier in the library, feeding dimes into the machine like he was playing slots, in search of a copy that didn't blur or show the incisions in the original.
<v Domingo Martinez>是托尼把我手中拿的第一把美工刀递给了我，那一刻，拿着那把小折刀，我感受到了巨大的可能性。当成绩单寄来时，我们会拦截它。然后托尼费尽心思地向我——他的跟班，一个几乎要变成现实的称呼——解释整个过程，在图书馆的复印机旁，像玩老虎机一样往机器里塞硬币，寻找一张没有模糊、没留下刀痕的复印件。

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:43.610
<v Domingo Martinez>Look, man, he said through his trendy and tinted John Lennon glasses, a wanker style even then, you've just got to remove the two from the 23 absences and then lighten the reproduction, and now you've got three absences in first period instead of 23. Now take the eight from the 48, move the four over, and put the eight in front of that, and now you have a B in Spanish instead of an F.
<v Domingo Martinez>听着，伙计，他戴着那种时髦的约翰·列侬眼镜说，即使在当时看来也是一种装腔作势的风格，你只需要把 23 次缺勤中的“2”去掉，然后调浅复印效果，现在第一节课的缺勤次数就从 23 次变成了 3 次。现在把 48 分中的“8”拿走，把“4”移过去，把“8”放到前面，现在你的西班牙语成绩就从 F 变成了 B。

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:54.280
<v Domingo Martinez>Oh, I said, in total understanding, a big smile growing on my face. Give a man a ride, he skips for a day. Teach him how to forge--
<v Domingo Martinez>哦，我说，完全理解，脸上露出了大大的笑容。给一个人搭车，他就能逃课一天。教他伪造——

00:10:54.280 --> 00:11:20.410
<v Domingo Martinez>It was ridiculously shortsighted, sure. But at that age, I never thought further than the immediate threat. Simply convincing my mother everything was quiet at school was enough for me. Dealing with school records and the larger consequences of robbing myself of even a substandard education, all that I would face at a later date-- and certainly have.
<v Domingo Martinez>这当然短视得可笑。但在那个年纪，我从没想过眼前的威胁之外的事。只要能让母亲确信学校里一切正常，对我来说就足够了。处理学校记录和剥夺自己受教育权带来的更严重的后果，所有这些我以后都会面对——而且确实面对了。

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:52.060
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony would usually borrow his mother's car for our expeditions, a blue Oldsmobile Delta 88. We'd leave school and drive to South Padre Island, a resort town at the end of a 28 mile highway that felt much more cosmopolitan than Brownsville, Texas, ever could. We did that drive, back and forth, three or four times a day, listening to Led Zeppelin, nodding our heads in unison with whomever else was stoned or drunk in the car.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼通常会借他母亲的车去探险，一辆蓝色的奥兹莫比尔 Delta 88。我们离开学校，驱车前往南帕德里岛，一个 28 英里公路尽头的度假小镇，它感觉比布朗斯维尔现代得多。我们来回跑，每天三四次，听着齐柏林飞艇乐队的音乐，和车里其他吸毒或喝醉的人一起点头。

00:11:52.060 --> 00:12:16.630
<v Domingo Martinez>My junior year and his senior year, Tony's parents bought him a Dodge Daytona. It was the year he would most assuredly graduate, they felt, and it was a chance for him to develop responsibility. In the mornings, the minute my mother would drop me off at school and disappear around the corner, Tony would drive around and park right in front of the school to pick me up, right in front of everybody. Dude, you gotta come skipping with me today, he'd say.
<v Domingo Martinez>高三那年，托尼高四，他父母给他买了一辆道奇戴托纳。他们觉得那一定是他毕业的一年，这是他培养责任感的机会。每天早上，我母亲送我到学校后刚一消失在拐角处，托尼就会开着车在学校门口转悠，把我接走，就在所有人面前。嘿，伙计，你今天得跟我去逃课，他说。

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:27.160
<v Domingo Martinez>But on one particular morning late in 1988, I balk. Nah, Tony. I gotta go back to class today, I protest. It's Thursday, and I haven't been since last week.
<v Domingo Martinez>但在 1988 年末的一个特殊早晨，我犹豫了。不，托尼。我今天得回去上课，我抗议道。今天是星期四，我从上周起就没去过了。

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:48.620
<v Domingo Martinez>Look, he says, I got two signed reentry slips. I can get you back in tomorrow or next week. It's not a problem. And I found a new place to get killer weed. Finding pot was always a problem, so when Tony said he found someone new, and it wasn't one of the morons who hung out by the tennis courts before school, I was intrigued.
<v Domingo Martinez>听着，他说，我有两张签了字的复课单。我明天或下周能让你回去。没问题。而且我找到了一个买高纯度大麻的新地方。找大麻一直是个问题，所以当托尼说他找到了新货源，而且不是那些在学校网球场旁游荡的白痴时，我很感兴趣。

00:12:48.620 --> 00:13:14.050
<v Domingo Martinez>We first drive to a housing project east of school, where a woman sold $2 quarts of Budweiser out of her living room from a cooler to anyone with money, no questions asked. We buy a couple of quarts and smoke the last half-joint Tony has on the way to his new killer weed supplier. I was getting a bit high when I began to recognize the route he was taking and was then thoroughly taken aback when he drove right into my grandmother's driveway.
<v Domingo Martinez>我们先开车去学校东边的一个廉价房区，那里有个女人从客厅的冷藏箱里卖 2 美元的百威啤酒，给任何有钱人，不问缘由。我们买了几瓶，在去他那个高纯度大麻供应商的路上一路上抽完了托尼剩下的半根烟。我开始有点上头了，当我认出他走的路线时，他直接开进了我祖母的车道，我彻底惊呆了。

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:32.810
<v Domingo Martinez>I couldn't understand why. It just didn't make sense. This was the same driveway my family Pontiac would regularly pull into after church on Sundays when I was growing up in the late 1970s-- my mother's mother's house in downtown Brownsville. I was, I think the term is, unnerved.
<v Domingo Martinez>我无法理解为什么。这根本说不通。这就是 70 年代末我长大后，周日去完教堂后我们家的庞蒂亚克车经常开进来的车道——我在布朗斯维尔市中心的祖母家。我觉得，我想这个词是，很不安。

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:47.200
<v Domingo Martinez>My two uncles, Johnny and Abel, were working on a '79 Camaro when Tony drives up and parks, hood to hood, with their car. The hood was up, and they were both leaning into the guts of the engine, their heads popped up like bearded biker prairie dogs.
<v Domingo Martinez>我的两个叔叔，约翰尼和阿贝尔，正在修理一辆 79 年款的科迈罗，这时托尼开过来，头对头地停在他们的车旁。引擎盖开着，他们两个都探进引擎里，脑袋像长胡子的草原土拨鼠一样露出来。

00:13:47.200 --> 00:14:05.200
<v Domingo Martinez>I sat frozen in the passenger seat, uncertain what to do next. Tony, noticing that I was startled, tells me to be cool, to chill out. These guys look mean, but they're all right. Anyways, he says as he's getting out of the driver's side, they're kind of dumb, but they got great weed.
<v Domingo Martinez>我僵在副驾驶座上，不知道接下来该怎么办。托尼注意到我受惊了，让我冷静，放松。这些家伙看起来很凶，但他们没问题。总之，他说着推开驾驶座门，他们有点傻，但他们有很棒的大麻。

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:24.490
<v Domingo Martinez>Didn't I know it. Abel and Johnny had a long history with local biker gangs, even a rumored affiliation with the Hell's Angels. They could get drugs nobody else could in this town. And as a result, they were total burnouts, hardly capable of cogent speech patterns in either English or Spanish. They landed in jail as often as other people attended church.
<v Domingo Martinez>我太知道了。阿贝尔和约翰尼与当地摩托车帮派有很长的历史，甚至有传闻说他们与地狱天使帮有联系。他们能搞到镇上其他人搞不到的毒品。结果，他们彻底堕落了，几乎没法进行连贯的英语或西班牙语对话。他们进监狱的频率和别人去教堂的频率差不多。

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:42.170
<v Domingo Martinez>But what they lacked in brains they certainly made up in brawn-- not that they'd tear apart a teenager like Tony or me, not in the daylight anyway. They had a code about that sort of thing. But if they felt cheated, they'd have taken a tire iron to my head long before they recognized me as their nephew. They were that burned out.
<v Domingo Martinez>但他们脑子里的缺失绝对是用蛮力弥补了——并不是说他们会像撕碎托尼或我这样的青少年，至少在大白天不会。他们对此有一套准则。但如果他们觉得被骗了，在认出我是他们外甥之前，他们早就用撬棍砸我的头了。他们已经堕落到那个地步了。

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:58.940
<v Domingo Martinez>So I sit there paralyzed in the front seat, side B of Houses of the Holy playing on Tony's cassette deck. And because it's hot, the AC is blasting. So once Tony closes the door, I can't hear anything. I just watch as this terrifying pantomime plays out before me.
<v Domingo Martinez>所以我瘫坐在前排座位上，播放着托尼卡带机里的《圣屋》B 面。因为很热，空调在狂吹。所以托尼关上门后，我什么也听不见。我只是看着眼前这出恐怖的哑剧上演。

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:13.490
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony, half-shaven in his preppy clothes, closes a door and hails his greeting. My uncle Abel, already brain dead from years of sniffing paint, narrows his eyes in suspicion and then noiselessly responds with a nodding. Hey.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼穿着预科生服装，半剃着胡子，关上车门大声打招呼。我的叔叔阿贝尔，因多年吸入油漆已经脑死亡，警惕地眯起眼睛，无声地回应地点了点头。嘿。

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:27.860
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony averts eye contact, looking anywhere but directly at Abel for fear that Abel might charge like a gorilla. Abel gives him a suspicious, quick upward jut of the chin that says, did I sell to you before? Who told you I got weed?
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼避开了眼神交流，看向别处，生怕阿贝尔像大猩猩一样冲过来。阿贝尔快速而怀疑地抬了下下巴，意思好像在说，我之前卖给你过吗？谁告诉你我有大麻？

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:45.890
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony lowers his head in quiet confidence, talking to Abel. Then my uncle Johnny nods towards me in the car, says something to Tony. They all turn to look at me. My eyes go wide, a big smile on my face, nodding. Tony says something, and then they all laugh together.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼低着头，小声而自信地和阿贝尔交谈。然后我叔叔约翰尼朝车里的我点点头，对托尼说了些什么。他们都转过头看着我。我睁大眼睛，脸上带着大大的笑容，点头回应。托尼说了些什么，然后他们一起大笑起来。

00:15:45.890 --> 00:16:10.710
<v Domingo Martinez>Led Zeppelin still plays loudly in the car. Then my uncle Abel slaps Tony on the back and leads   him around to the back of the car, right where I am. Johnny stands there too, looking at me and smiling, makes his index finger and thumb into a mock roach, and laughs. I mimic the roach back. Even now, he doesn't recognize me.
<v Domingo Martinez>齐柏林飞艇的音乐在车里依然大声播放。然后我叔叔阿贝尔拍了拍托尼的背，把他带到车后，就在我坐的地方。约翰尼也站在那，看着我微笑，把食指和拇指比作一个假装的烟头，大笑起来。我学着他的样子回敬他。即使到了现在，他还没认出我来。

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:28.890
<v Domingo Martinez>Then Tony and Abel emerge around the other side of the car with Tony's hand in his pocket, and both of them are laughing like they're suddenly old friends. Tony turns and waves, and both Johnny and Abel wave back. The door opens, and Tony says, dude, we got a big joint for $2, as he gets in the driver's seat.
<v Domingo Martinez>然后托尼和阿贝尔从车另一侧冒出来，托尼的手插在口袋里，两人笑得像突然成了老朋友。托尼转过身挥手，约翰尼和阿贝尔也挥手回应。门开了，托尼坐进驾驶座说，伙计，我们花 2 美元搞到了一根大烟卷。

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:47.140
<v Domingo Martinez>This has freaked me out to no end. Abel and Johnny are both waving, making the universal roach-smoking signal as we drive off, and it leaves me feeling really, really conflicted. The car slips up the southernmost terminus of Highway 77, and we head north from urban Brownsville to drive around as we smoke the joint.
<v Domingo Martinez>这彻底吓坏我了。我们开走时，阿贝尔和约翰尼都在挥手，比着那个全世界通用的抽烟手势，这让我感到非常非常矛盾。车滑向 77 号公路的最南端终点，我们往北开，一边绕行一边抽那个烟卷。

00:16:47.140 --> 00:17:01.580
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony lights it, and it starts burning purple. Purple Haze, he says, and then follows it with his characteristic, ahh!
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼把它点着了，它开始冒紫烟。紫色阴霾，他说，然后接着他标志性的笑声，啊！

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:18.740
<v Domingo Martinez>Hey man, I say, I'm kind of scared about smoking this. I've never seen one burn this color. Aw dude, says Tony, don't worry about it. Those guys got killer weed, man. They're, like, bikers or something. It's probably laced with something. That's why it was $2.
<v Domingo Martinez>嘿，伙计，我说，我有点害怕抽这个。我从没见过烟冒这种颜色。噢伙计，托尼说，别担心。那些家伙有高质量的大麻。他们是摩托车手什么的。这可能是掺了什么东西。这就是为什么它只要 2 美元。

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:26.360
<v Domingo Martinez>This idea sounds appealing to Tony. It scares the [BLEEP] out of me. We're both getting incredibly high.
<v Domingo Martinez>这个主意对托尼来说很有吸引力。但我被吓惨了。我们俩都变得兴奋到了极点。

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:41.460
<v Domingo Martinez>Hey man, says Tony, wouldn't it be messed up if, like, when you were high, your hair went into, like, a huge orange Afro, and the higher you were, the bigger your Afro got? You couldn't go anywhere, because people would be like, man, that guy's stoned.
<v Domingo Martinez>嘿，伙计，托尼说，如果在你嗨的时候，你的头发变成一个巨大的橙色爆炸头，而且你越嗨，爆炸头就越大，那会怎么样？你哪儿也去不了，因为人们会说，伙计，那家伙嗨大了。

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:58.520
<v Domingo Martinez>I sit there in Tony's car and think about my uncles, Johnny and Abel. Johnny had been stabbed in the back with a flat-headed screwdriver about a month earlier in a street fight. His lung had been punctured, and my grandmother said you could hear whistling every time he inhaled. He wouldn't go to the hospital to get it treated for three days.
<v Domingo Martinez>我坐在托尼的车里，想起我的叔叔约翰尼和阿贝尔。约翰尼一个月前在街头斗殴中背部被一把平头螺丝刀刺伤。他的肺部被刺穿了，我祖母说你能听到他每次吸气时都有哨声。他坚持了三天都没去医院治疗。

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:10.050
<v Domingo Martinez>We're halfway done with the joint when I say to Tony, hey man, I don't want to get stoned anymore. All right, well, put it out, Tony says, nodding his head back and forth to Zeppelin.
<v Domingo Martinez>我们抽了一半烟时，我对托尼说，嘿，伙计，我不想再嗨了。好吧，那把它掐掉吧，托尼说，随着齐柏林飞艇的音乐摇头晃脑。

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:24.140
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony's left hand is fingering chords into the neck of an imaginary guitar as he's driving. I watch his fingers move for a few seconds, suspended and twisting around like they're an overturned King crab, and I can find no correlation with the chords in the song.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼的左手在开车时假装弹奏吉他，手指在指板上舞动。我看着他的手指晃动了几秒钟，悬空扭动，就像一只翻过来的帝王蟹，我找不到这和歌里的和弦有任何联系。

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:44.280
<v Domingo Martinez>Man, I mean I don't want to smoke pot anymore, I say to him. I don't want to skip class anymore. I want to get back to school. Not today, but, like, in general. I don't want to feel like this anymore, like I'm doing something bad. I feel like this all the time now, dirty.
<v Domingo Martinez>伙计，我是说我不想再抽大麻了，我对他讲。我不想再逃课了。我想回到学校。不是今天，而是，总的来说。我不想再有这种感觉了，像我在做坏事。我现在一直都有这种感觉，很脏。

00:18:44.280 --> 00:19:00.250
<v Domingo Martinez>Look at that really [BLEEP] small house over there. We were on an overpass, and I just noticed a house beneath us in the Brownsville Country Club, about a quarter of the size of the houses surrounding it. Tony starts laughing so hard I have to make him focus back on the driving, but then I laugh along with him.
<v Domingo Martinez>看那边那栋非常[哔]小的房子。我们在立交桥上，我刚注意到我们下方布朗斯维尔乡村俱乐部里的一栋房子，只有周围房子四分之一那么大。托尼笑得太厉害了，我不得不让他集中精神开车，但随后我也跟着他笑了起来。

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:09.510
<v Domingo Martinez>You're stoned, he tells me. Yeah, I say. I'm way stoned.
<v Domingo Martinez>你嗨了，他告诉我。是啊，我说。我嗨透了。

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:26.710
<v Domingo Martinez>Hey, man, I say a little later-- we're driving back to South Padre Island now-- you know those guys we bought weed from earlier today? The bikers, Tony says. That was my grandmother's house, man. Those are my uncles, I say, even though I'm really embarrassed by it.
<v Domingo Martinez>嘿，伙计，过了一会儿我说——我们现在正开回南帕德里岛——你知道我们今天早些时候买大麻的那家伙吗？那些摩托车手，托尼说。那是我祖母的房子，伙计。那些是我叔叔，我说，尽管我为此感到非常尴尬。

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:37.690
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony finds this befuddling. He can't figure out what the bikers were doing at my grandmother's house. Those dudes were my mom's brothers, man, my uncles, I explain.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼觉得这很令人困惑。他搞不懂为什么那些摩托车手会在我祖母家。伙计，那些是我妈的兄弟，是我叔叔，我解释道。

00:19:37.690 --> 00:20:10.110
<v Domingo Martinez>Tony is laughing so hard he has to pull over to the side of the road. His laughing is infectious, and I find myself laughing right along with him, laughing harder than I have laughed in a really, really long time. But I'm feeling utterly beyond redemption on the inside, like I had just done something today that I couldn't take back, like my course was now set.
<v Domingo Martinez>托尼笑得不得不把车停在路边。他的笑声很有感染力，我发现自己也跟着他大笑起来，笑得比我很久很久以来笑得都开心。但在内心深处，我感到一种彻底无法救赎的感觉，好像我今天做了一件无法挽回的事，好像我的人生轨迹从此定型了。

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:21.250
<v Nancy Updike>Domingo Martinez, reading a story from his memoir, The Boy Kings of Texas . He's got a new book coming out this fall called My Heart is a Drunken Compass .
<v Nancy Updike>多明戈·马丁内斯，朗读他的回忆录《德州的男孩国王》中的一个片段。他今年秋天要出一本新书，叫《我的心是醉酒的指南针》。

00:20:21.250 --> 00:21:15.750
<v Nancy Updike>[MUSIC - "WRONG CROWD" BY PRINCE GEORGE]
<v Nancy Updike>[音乐 - “错误的群体” 作者：乔治王子]

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:37.350
<v Nancy Updike>Act Two, One Life to Live. Having a blind spot about yourself is not the end of the world, depending on what it is and how big it is. Sometimes that's all hard to tell, though, without outside eyes. This next story we changed some of the names. It's a complicated situation that's still unfolding.
<v Nancy Updike>第二幕，仅剩的人生。对自己有盲点并不是世界末日，具体取决于那是什么以及它有多大。不过，没有旁人的视角，有时很难判断。对于下一个故事，我们改动了一些名字。这是一个复杂且仍在发展的情况。

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:52.230
<v Nancy Updike>Sarah got into a bad car accident last year. She was in a coma with a traumatic brain injury, and her doctors thought she might die. But after 52 days in the coma, she woke up. Our producer Miki Meek tells the story of what happened next.
<v Nancy Updike>莎拉去年出了一场严重的车祸。她陷入昏迷，大脑严重受损，医生以为她可能会死。但在昏迷 52 天后，她醒了过来。我们的制作人米基·米克讲述了接下来的故事。

00:21:52.230 --> 00:22:13.280
<v Miki Meek>Waking up from a coma doesn't happen all at once. It's a slow process, like coming awake over months, sometimes even years. For Sarah, it took almost three months before she could talk well enough to have a simple conversation and another four months for her to get out of the hospital and go home to live with her parents. She still needed a lot of care, but she was really excited to spend time with her husband Billy. He had a birthday party.
<v Miki Meek>从昏迷中醒来不是瞬间发生的。这是一个缓慢的过程，就像在几个月甚至几年里慢慢醒来一样。对莎拉来说，花了近三个月的时间，她才能说话进行简单的对话，又花了四个月才出院回家，和父母住在一起。她仍然需要很多照料，但她真的很高兴能和丈夫比利待在一起。他有一个生日派对。

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:34.370
<v Sarah>And that's when I just said, will you give me a kiss, please? And he was like, mm-- maybe I can give you one of these. And he turned my head and kissed me on the cheek. So I was hurt and kind of frustrated and annoyed.
<v Sarah>然后我就说了，你能吻我一下吗，拜托？他当时就像，嗯——也许我可以给你这个。他转过我的头，亲了我的脸颊。所以我感到受伤，有点沮丧和恼火。

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:40.990
<v Miki Meek>Sarah thought maybe Billy was just worried about getting her sick, so she made a comment about it to her stepmom Alice when they got in the car to go home.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉以为比利只是担心传染她生病，所以当他们坐车回家时，她跟继母爱丽丝说了这件事。

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:50.390
<v Sarah>I said, I think that Billy is worried he'll get me sick because he won't kiss me. And that's when she said--
<v Sarah>我说，我觉得比利担心会传染我，因为他不肯亲我。然后她说了——

00:22:50.390 --> 00:23:10.060
<v Alice>We need to talk. And that's when I had the impression she was ready. I said, it feels different. Being with Billy, it feels different. She's like, yeah, it does. I was, like, well, that's because you guys aren't married anymore. You're divorced.
<v Alice>我们需要谈谈。那一刻，我觉得她准备好了。我说，感觉不对劲。和比利在一起，感觉不一样了。她说，是啊，确实不对劲。我说，哦，那是因为你们已经不再是夫妻了。你们离婚了。

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:29.740
<v Miki Meek>Sarah had forgotten the entire two years before her accident. She'd forgotten the fact that during those two years she had fallen out of love with her husband and divorced him. Here's what she did remember-- she remembered her wedding day in 1998. She was 17, and Billy was 20. She had just found out she was pregnant with twins.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉忘记了事故发生前整整两年的事情。她忘记了在这两年里，她已经不再爱丈夫并和他离婚的事实。她记得的是——她记得 1998 年的结婚那天。她 17 岁，比利 20 岁。她刚刚发现自己怀了双胞胎。

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:45.090
<v Miki Meek>Sarah remembered the house where their family grew from two kids to four. It was in a small town out west, just down the street from her parents. She remembered family camping trips on the weekends, baseball games, and dinner together every night-- 13 years of memories like that.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉记得他们家庭从两个孩子增加到四个孩子的那个家。它在西部的一个小镇上，离她父母家只有几条街。她记得周末的家庭露营，棒球比赛，还有每天晚上的晚餐——像那样的 13 年回忆。

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:54.840
<v Miki Meek>It wasn't always perfect. Billy joined the National Guard and was gone a lot. He served in Iraq. Sometimes he had a bad temper. But they loved each other.
<v Miki Meek>并不总是完美的。比利参加了国民警卫队，经常不在家。他在伊拉克服役。有时他的脾气很坏。但他们彼此相爱。

00:23:54.840 --> 00:24:10.410
<v Miki Meek>There was a lot more to the story, and her family really wanted to sit her down and fill all those memories back in for her. But the doctors told them they had to be cautious. Let things come up naturally for Sarah. Let her brain set the pace. That was the best way for her to recover.
<v Miki Meek>故事远不止于此，她的家人真的很想坐下来，把所有的记忆都补回给她。但医生告诉他们必须谨慎。让莎拉自然地想起。让她的脑子自己定节奏。那是她康复的最佳方式。

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:17.080
<v Miki Meek>So when her stepmom told her about her divorce, Sarah was shocked and confused. And for her, there was only one question.
<v Miki Meek>所以当继母告诉她离婚的事时，莎拉震惊且困惑。对她来说，只有一个问题。

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:22.150
<v Sarah>Do you think it's too late? Do you think I can get him back?
<v Sarah>你觉得太晚了吗？你觉得我还能追回他吗？

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:26.170
<v Miki Meek>The next day, Sarah called up Billy and asked him to come over.
<v Miki Meek>第二天，莎拉打电话叫比利过来。

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:44.820
<v Sarah>And I just flat out said, my stepmom says that we got divorced, and I'm hoping that you'll take me back. And when I first said it, then he did smile. But then he got kind of stern and more serious.
<v Sarah>我就直截了当地说了，我继母说我们离婚了，但我希望你能跟我复婚。当我刚说出口时，他确实笑了。但后来他变得有点严厉和严肃了。

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:52.170
<v Billy>And I told her, well, I need to take my time.
<v Billy>我对她说，好吧，我需要慢慢来。

00:24:52.170 --> 00:25:10.260
<v Miki Meek>When one person loses two years, it forces everyone else around them to go back in time too. Billy had never wanted the divorce, but he understood why Sarah left him. She had come to despise him during those two years she had forgotten. And now here was the old Sarah again, the one who still loved him, calling him regularly.
<v Miki Meek>当一个人失去了两年记忆，这迫使周围所有的人也跟着回到过去。比利从来都不想要离婚，但他理解莎拉为什么要离开他。在她忘记的那两年里，她已经开始鄙视他了。现在，那个旧的莎拉又回来了，那个依然爱他的莎拉，经常打电话给他。

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:20.300
<v Billy>Like, hey Billy, you know, it's really good to hear your voice. And I was like, well, that's awesome. It's really good to hear you too, you know?
<v Billy>就像，嘿比利，你知道，听到你的声音真好。我说，哇，太棒了。听到你的声音也很好，你知道吗？

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:40.380
<v Billy>She was like, Billy, I love you so much. The "so" was  always-- that stood out. Yeah, it was weird. I'm not going to lie to you, it was like, oh my goodness. This was tough. I was in a spot where I'm like, oh, why? Why are you telling me this?
<v Billy>她会说，比利，我太爱你了。那个“太”字总是——很突出。是啊，很奇怪。我不骗你，当时我的感觉就像，哦，天哪。这太难了。我当时处在一个像，哦，为什么？你为什么要跟我说这个？

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:51.880
<v Billy>This is not what I want to hear, you know. And then the other part of me is telling me, like, yes, this is what I want to hear.
<v Billy>这不是我想听的，你知道。然后我内心的另一部分在告诉我，比如，是的，这就是我想听的。

00:25:51.880 --> 00:26:03.130
<v Miki Meek>As much as Billy wanted to hear it, the rest of Sarah's family did not. She was falling back in love with a guy she wanted nothing to do with before her accident. Her sister Jessica remembers watching all of this with alarm.
<v Miki Meek>尽管比利很想听，但莎拉的其他家人却不想。她正在重新爱上一个她在事故前根本不想再有任何瓜葛的家伙。她的妹妹杰西卡记得惊恐地看着这一切。

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:30.680
<v Jessica>She would get this little schoolgirl look on her face and, like, bat her eyes, like a little teenager looking at their crush. And she's like, hi, Billy! And so I would just look at her so disgusted, just sitting there, zipping my mouth closed so that I don't say, you are divorced. You are divorced for a reason. Like, stop batting your eyes at him. You don't like him.
<v Jessica>她脸上会露出那种小学生的表情，眨着眼睛，就像个看着暗恋对象的青春期女孩。她会说，嗨，比利！所以我只会厌恶地看着她，闭紧嘴巴，不让自己说，你们离婚了。你们离婚是有原因的。别再对他眨眼了。你根本不喜欢他。

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:51.260
<v Miki Meek>Sarah's family remembered very clearly what Sarah herself did not, the many reasons why she had left Billy, why she was living with them now, not him. Her daughter Kristen recalled getting into an argument with her dad. The situation escalated, like it always did. As Kristen was telling me the story, she pointed to a patched up hole in the basement wall.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉的家人非常清楚莎拉自己不记得的事，她离开比利的许多原因，她为什么现在和他们住在一起，而不是和他住在一起。她的女儿克里斯汀记得和爸爸吵过架。情况总是会升级。当克里斯汀给我讲这个故事时，她指了指地下室墙上一个修补过的洞。

00:26:51.260 --> 00:27:08.300
<v Kristen>That hole in the wall was my mom's breaking point. My dad tried to swing at me, and he barely missed my head. And I just remember my mom was so quiet that night. Like, she didn't want to go upstairs. She didn't want to go find my dad. She was scared.
<v Kristen>墙上的那个洞是我妈妈的爆发点。我爸爸想打我，他差点没打到我的头。我只记得那天晚上我妈妈很安静。就像，她不想上楼。她不想去找我爸爸。她很害怕。

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:28.570
<v Kristen>And she looked  at the wall, and she was looking at me. And I was sitting on the ground crying. I yelled at her that night, divorce him. And one day she wrote him a letter, and when he came home from work, he found the letter that said, you need to pack up your stuff and leave. My mom had had it.
<v Kristen>她看着墙，然后看着我。我坐在地上哭。那天晚上我冲她吼，和他离婚。有一天她给他写了一封信，当他下班回家时，他发现了那封信，上面写着，你必须收拾行李离开。我妈受够了。

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:45.940
<v Miki Meek>Sarah didn't remember this moment of Billy punching a hole in the wall until she had a conversation with her bishop at church. She and Billy had gone to this bishop for counseling when they were married. Now she was going back to him on a detective mission of sorts. She knew she had left Billy, but maybe the bishop could help her remember why.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉不记得比利在墙上打洞的那个时刻，直到她和教堂的主教谈话。他们在婚姻期间曾找这位主教做咨询。现在她正作为某种侦探任务回去找他。她知道她离开了比利，但也许主教能帮她回忆起原因。

00:27:45.940 --> 00:28:10.490
<v Sarah>And he was like, you were tired. You were so tired of the emotional abuse. He punched a hole in the wall. And I remembered it instantly. And I thought, oh yeah, I can remember that. And it makes sense as to why I would have divorced him with all the things we were dealing with.
<v Sarah>他说，你累了。你对情感虐待感到太累了。他在墙上打了个洞。我立刻就想起来了。我想，哦对了，我能记得那个。而且考虑到我们处理的所有事情，我为什么会和他离婚也就说得通了。

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:26.720
<v Miki Meek>So this is what Sarah's life is like right now. Memories of her and Billy's bad times are starting to pop back into her mind. Some are big, like the time he threw scissors at her and cut her under her eye. Others are small. There's no particular order to them. She says she gets a new memory every two weeks or so.
<v Miki Meek>这就是莎拉现在的生活状态。关于她和比利糟糕时光的记忆开始在她脑海中浮现。有些是大事，比如他向她扔剪刀，划伤她眼睛下方。有些是小事。它们没有特定的顺序。她说她大约每两周就会有一个新的记忆。

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:53.180
<v Sarah>When it comes to me, it's just like a light gets switched on in my head. So it goes from darkness and nothing being there to all of a sudden something is there. Like, a recent one is that he would take my phone and read through my phone all the time because he thought that the only reason I wanted a divorce was because I wanted to date other men.
<v Sarah>对于我来说，就像脑子里开了一盏灯。所以它从黑暗和空无一物，突然之间就有了东西。比如，最近一个记忆是，他总是拿走我的手机翻看，因为他认为我想要离婚的唯一原因是我想要和别的男人约会。

00:28:53.180 --> 00:29:05.130
<v Miki Meek>While we were talking, another memory appeared right in front of me. This one was about how by the end of their marriage, they were going out of their way just to be mean to each other. Sarah would tell Billy all the time that she liked her friends better than him.
<v Miki Meek>在我们谈话时，另一个记忆就在我面前出现了。这一次是关于在婚姻结束时，他们是如何费尽心机去伤害对方的。莎拉总是告诉比利，她喜欢朋友胜过喜欢他。

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:24.390
<v Sarah>I think I used to tell him that my friends were number one and that he was number two. And so I was definitely hurting his feelings. That was a memory that just barely came-- like, just now while we were talking.
<v Sarah>我想我以前常告诉他，我的朋友是第一位，而他是第二位。所以我是绝对伤害了他的感情。那是刚刚才浮现出的记忆——就在刚才我们聊天的时候。

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:42.740
<v Miki Meek>Sarah's biggest problem is that she knows her marriage got bad. She knows those facts. But she just can't remember the feelings-- the feelings of being so fed up that she stopped wanting to work it out with Billy. Not being able to access those feelings is one reason it's so hard to accept the decision she made back then. But there's another layer to the story.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉最大的问题在于她知道婚姻变糟糕了。她知道那些事实。但她就是无法想起那些感觉——那种受够了，不再想和比利解决问题的感觉。无法触及那些感觉是她很难接受当时那个决定的原因之一。但故事还有另一面。

00:29:42.740 --> 00:30:00.270
<v Miki Meek>That guy she wanted to divorce has, by all accounts, changed. The before and after in Sarah's life was the accident. In Billy's it was the divorce. He was devastated by their break-up. He started reading self-help books, going to church, and for a while he was seeing three different counselors.
<v Miki Meek>那个她想要离婚的家伙，据各方面所说，已经变了。莎拉生活的前后分界线是事故。比利的则是离婚。他对他们的分手感到崩溃。他开始读自助书籍，去教堂，有一段时间他同时看三个不同的咨询师。

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:07.440
<v Billy>Everything was just angry. It was just-- I was always mad. So I would just react to everything.
<v Billy>一切都只有愤怒。就是——我总是很生气。所以我对一切都反应过激。

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:12.590
<v Miki Meek>You know, some of the stuff that just sort of came up is, you know, grabbing your daughter and putting her up against the wall and punching a hole.
<v Miki Meek>你知道，一些刚浮现出来的事情是，比如，抓住女儿把她推到墙上并打个洞。

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:27.920
<v Billy>I'll tell you this-- I will take full responsibility for that. I shouldn't have punched a wall. I shouldn't have, and I did. Of course that makes me regret it. I feel bad. I feel horrible. I feel-- I wish I would have listened more.
<v Billy>我告诉你——我会对此负全部责任。我不该打墙。我不该，但我确实做了。当然这让我后悔。我感到难过。我感到糟糕。我感到——我真希望我当时能多听听。

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:41.410
<v Billy>I wish I would have got the help I needed and admitted. And after I lost everything, that's the only time I really refocused and thought, wait a minute, I lost it all. Now I've got  to start over.
<v Billy>我真希望我当时能得到我需要的帮助并承认这一点。失去一切之后，那才是我真正重新审视并想，等等，我失去了一切。现在我必须从头开始。

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:56.340
<v Miki Meek>Billy did start over, mainly by repairing his relationship with his kids. They've been living with Billy full-time since Sarah's accident, and it's going well. Their daughter Kristen says Billy's like a different person. He listens more now and wants to talk problems out.
<v Miki Meek>比利确实从头开始了，主要是通过修复他和孩子们的关系。莎拉事故后，孩子们一直和比利全职住在一起，情况进行得很好。他们的女儿克里斯汀说比利简直换了一个人。他现在听得更多，也想把问题谈开。

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:14.490
<v Kristen>Me and my dad are really close. Throughout time, after him and my mom got divorced, he figured out ways to control his anger. I mean, my dad's really good at keeping calm-- now. Now he is. And he's really good at handling situations with us.
<v Kristen>我和我爸真的很亲。随着时间的推移，在他和我妈离婚后，他找到了控制自己愤怒的方法。我是说，我爸现在真的很擅长保持冷静。现在是的。他很擅长处理我们之间的情况。

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:29.730
<v Kristen>And so I'm not saying I'm glad my mom wrecked. But I wouldn't trade anything because I like where we are now, because my dad wouldn't be living with us right now if she didn't wreck. And so I feel like I got to know my dad so much better.
<v Kristen>所以我不是说我很高兴我妈出车祸了。但我什么都不会换，因为我喜欢我们现在的状态，因为如果她没出事，我爸现在就不会和我们住在一起。所以我觉得我更了解我爸爸了。

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:35.160
<v Miki Meek>Is there any part of you that sometimes thinks, oh, maybe if they could stay this way, then maybe it  would work?
<v Miki Meek>你内心有没有哪部分会想，哦，也许如果他们能保持这样，那么也许行得通？

00:31:35.160 --> 00:32:09.510
<v Kristen>So everything's really good between them right now. But I don't think that they'd be good as a married couple. And I probably won't ever think that, because I've seen the worst of them, and I've seen the best of them. And I still think that the worst of them has more control. Because what if they do go back to old habits and my dad does start to get mean again? I feel like it'd ruin what we built while she was gone.
<v Kristen>所以他们现在一切都很好。但我认为他们不适合做夫妻。而且我可能永远不会那样想，因为我看过他们最糟糕的一面，也看过他们最好的一面。我依然认为最糟糕的一面占据更多控制权。因为如果他们又回到旧习惯，我爸又开始变坏了呢？我觉得这会毁了她在的时候我们建立的东西。

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:23.640
<v Miki Meek>A few nights a week, Sarah and Billy go for these little drives around town in his truck and just talk about what it might be like if they got back together. They pass the church they went to when they were married and the park where they took their kids to play. These drives are like dates for them.
<v Miki Meek>一周几个晚上，莎拉和比利会开着他的卡车在镇上兜兜风，谈谈如果他们复合会是什么样子。他们经过他们结婚时去过的教堂，和他们带孩子去玩的公园。这些车程对他们来说就像约会。

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:37.690
<v Sarah>Kinda nice how things have gone. So it's like we get to start over, you know? It would be better for the kids if we could learn how to be a team. And I wasn't very good at being a team with you.
<v Sarah>事情进行得真不错。所以就像我们有了重新开始的机会，你知道吗？如果我们能学会如何成为一个团队，这对孩子们会更好。但我以前不太擅长和你成为一个团队。

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:40.310
<v Billy>Neither was I.
<v Billy>我也不擅长。

00:32:40.310 --> 00:33:03.200
<v Miki Meek>Sarah's 33. Billy's 36. And they know everyone else thinks it would be crazy for them to get back together again. And it is crazy-- soap opera crazy. Sarah gets amnesia and forgets that she hated her husband so much that she divorced him. Meanwhile, the husband her daughter begged her to divorce has taken Sarah's place in the house. He's now the person who takes care of the children. He's even someone they trust.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉 33 岁。比利 36 岁。他们知道其他所有人都认为他们复合会很疯狂。而且这确实很疯狂——像肥皂剧一样疯狂。莎拉失忆了，忘记了她曾经非常恨丈夫以至于和他离婚。与此同时，她女儿求她离婚的那个丈夫已经取代了莎拉在家的位置。他现在是照看孩子的人。他甚至成了孩子们信任的人。

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:20.140
<v Sarah>Kind of feels like I was supposed to get in the wreck, because we learned a lot. Because I woke up not even remembering I was divorced. And so I had all those months in the hospital just thinking of how much I love you and how much I miss you, and--
<v Sarah>感觉好像我本来就该出这场车祸，因为我们学到了很多。因为我醒来时甚至不记得我离婚了。所以我整整几个月在医院里只是想着我是多么爱你，我是多么想你，而且——

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:29.290
<v Billy>I was trying so hard to always work on not loving you. It didn't work.
<v Billy>我一直在努力不去爱你。这没用。

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:48.770
<v Miki Meek>Couples who split up are usually facing a pile of bad memories so huge that there's no hope of digging their way out. Trying to talk about one wrongdoing sets off this chain reaction of anger and bitterness about all the other wrongdoings. But Sarah's accident lets her and Billy deal with one memory at a time, as they bubble up, usually in the mornings when she first wakes up.
<v Miki Meek>分手的情侣通常面临一堆堆糟糕的记忆，大到没有挖出来的希望。试图谈论其中一件错事会引发关于其他所有错事的愤怒和痛苦的连锁反应。但莎拉的事故让她和比利可以一次处理一个记忆，随着它们冒出来，通常是在她刚醒来的早晨。

00:33:48.770 --> 00:34:01.020
<v Sarah>Sometimes I just lay in bed. And then the first person I talk to-- because I always decide when I have a memory, I've decided to bring it up to Billy every time.
<v Sarah>有时我只是躺在床上。然后我谈话的第一个人——因为我总是决定当我有了记忆，我就决定每次都跟比利提一下。

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:04.050
<v Miki Meek>And then how has he been responding to those?
<v Miki Meek>那他对此有什么反应？

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:13.550
<v Sarah>He's been good about it, just admitting what happened and apologizing and telling me that he'll be better.
<v Sarah>他一直做得很好，只是承认发生了什么并道歉，告诉我他会变好。

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:17.680
<v Miki Meek>And one thing I was wondering, when you guys were married, did you ever bring this up to him?
<v Miki Meek>还有一件事我很好奇，你们结婚的时候，你有没有提过这些？

00:34:17.680 --> 00:34:42.680
<v Sarah>Not when we were married. Uh-uh. He used to would have never apologized for anything that he had done. And the good thing about me losing my memory is that we're able to talk about everything that comes up in a better way. It's less hurt and less powerful, you know what I mean?
<v Sarah>我们结婚时没有。不。他过去从来不会为他做过的任何事道歉。我失忆的好处是，我们能够以更好的方式谈论所有冒出来的事。少了一些伤害，也没那么有杀伤力，你知道我的意思吗？

00:34:42.680 --> 00:34:58.940
<v Miki Meek>Sarah's changed too. She says she gets irritated easier now and can throw these tantrums she never had before. She's still learning to get around without a walker and to do small things, like organize her pills for the day. It's not clear when she can move out of her parents' house. They have legal guardianship over her.
<v Miki Meek>莎拉也变了。她说她现在更容易生气，会发以前从没发过的脾气。她还在学习在没有助行器的情况下四处走动，做些小事，比如整理她一天的药片。不清楚她什么时候能从父母家搬走。他们拥有她的法定监护权。

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:18.590
<v Miki Meek>And she goes back and forth about Billy. One day she told me, I'd marry that guy tomorrow if he asked. And other times, she's less sure. She knows divorcing Billy was the right decision back then. She just wants to make the right decision now.
<v Miki Meek>她对比利反反复复。有一天她对我说，如果他开口，我明天就会嫁给他。而其他时候，她就不那么确定了。她知道当时和比利离婚是正确的决定。她只是想做出现在的正确决定。

00:35:18.590 --> 00:35:39.500
<v Nancy Updike>Miki Meek is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, what the movie The Breakfast Club can teach you about parenting if you were in The Breakfast Club . That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Nancy Updike>米基·米克是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，如果看过电影《早餐俱乐部》，它能教你关于为人父母的道理。这在芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台的节目继续后一分钟播出。

00:35:39.500 --> 00:36:00.980
<v Nancy Updike>It's This American Life . I'm Nancy Updike, sitting in for Ira Glass who will be back next week. Every week, we choose a theme-- you know that-- and we present various stories on that theme. Today's show, "Is That What I Look Like?" Stories of seeing yourself through other people's eyes, whether you want to or not. And we're here at Act Three, which I think is going to be kind of a two-parter.
<v Nancy Updike>这里是《美国生活》。我是南希·厄普代克，为下周就要回来的艾拉·格拉斯代班。每周，我们都会选定一个主题——你知道的——然后我们呈现关于那个主题的各种故事。今天的节目是“我看起来是那个样子吗？”关于通过别人的眼睛看待自己的故事，无论你是否愿意。我们现在进行到第三幕，我想这会是一个两段式的部分。

